{"/api/discogs/artist/180922/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"180922","image":"https://i.discogs.com/7NyWhSO9-Fbla2jCEyI5ExXq3yQ9s2VDt9itO7p12Xw/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:534/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTE4MDky/Mi0xNTM4MzM5Mjkz/LTgyNzcuanBlZw.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/Uc7UYz5KxTgH1k-eKC7H0_9TIeUSDs_jCB6gcK0KbG0/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTE4MDky/Mi0xNTM4MzM5Mjkz/LTgyNzcuanBlZw.jpeg","profile":"American soul / jazz / R&B singer / songwriter. Born May 2nd, 1954 in the Bronx, New York City, NY, U.S.A. Died June 13th, 2024 in Vallejo, CA, U.S.A.\r\n\r\nDueted with [a=Johnny Mathis] for the ballad \"You're A Special Part Of Me\" (1984). Her signature song was \"I Try\" but also recorded dance hits \"Too Tough\", \"Angel Of The Night\" and \"Something About You\".","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:180922","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":[{"active":true,"id":303187,"name":"Ricardo Marrero & The Group"}],"members":null,"namevariations":["A Bofil","A. Bofill","Angel Bofill","Angela \"Super Rock Bitch\" Bofill","Angela \"Super-Rock-Bitch\" Bofill","Angela Bo'ill","Angela Bofield","Angela Bofill (Resident Diva)","Angela Tomasa Bofill","Angie B.","Angie Boffil","Angie Bofill","Bofill"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/180922-Angela-Bofill","type":"artist","urls":["http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angela_Bofill","https://www.imdb.com/name/nm3494044/","http://www.whosampled.com/Angela-Bofill/"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/196766/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"196766","image":"https://i.discogs.com/cPeOC8iVBQRxRSqbogNxhEcH5oQ9G-pMkdL2UTKk4Jc/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:410/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTE5Njc2/Ni0xNTk1OTY4MTMx/LTIyMjEuanBlZw.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/XjfCGUSH52vHiRj-zfH3H47D3Hc8G64a52iP91J5oCQ/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTE5Njc2/Ni0xNTk1OTY4MTMx/LTIyMjEuanBlZw.jpeg","profile":"English rock band formed in Shepherd's Bush, London, England, in 1964.\n\nKnown for their involvement in the 1960s British mod movement, the smashing of their instruments, their development of the rock opera genre, the integration of synthesizers and the development of the \"Marshall Stack\" amplifier design. They set the Guinness world record on May 31, 1976, for loudest concert, at 126 decibels. Inducted into Rock & Roll Hall Of Fame in 1990 (Performer).\n\nFormed in March 1959 as The Detours by Roger Daltrey. John Entwistle joined in September 1961 and Pete Townshend in February 1962. In February 1964 they changed their name to \"The Who\" but after Keith Moon replaced previous drummers Doug Sandom and Dave Golding their then-current manager [a=Peter Meaden] renamed the band [a=The High Numbers] in April 1964. \n\nIn November 1964, the name was changed back to \"The Who.\" Keith Moon died following the release of Who Are You. He was replaced by Kenney Jones. Rabbit Bundrick joined shortly afterwards. The band broke up in 1983 but reunited in July 1985 for Live Aid and various one-off occasions until releasing a new album in 2006. In 2025 they undertook another North American farewell tour.\n\n[b]Members[/b]\n[i]As The Detours/Dale Angelo and the Detours[/i]\nColin Dawson – lead vocals (1959–1963)\nGabriel \"Gabby\" Connolly – lead vocals (1963)\nRoger Daltrey – lead guitar (1959–1961), rhythm guitar (1961–1963), lead vocals (1963–1964)\nPeter James – rhythm guitar (1961–1962)\nReg Bowen – rhythm guitar (1962)\nRoy Ellis – rhythm guitar (1961–1962)\nJohn Entwistle – bass guitar, horns (1961–1964)\nPete Townshend – rhythm guitar (1962–1963) lead guitar (1964)\nHarry Wilson – drums (1961–1962)\nDoug Sandom – drums (1962–1964)\n\n[i]Founding members:[/i]\nRoger Daltrey – lead vocals\nPete Townshend – guitar, vocals, keyboards\nJohn Entwistle – bass guitar, horns, vocals (1964–2002)\n\n[i]Former members:[/i]\nDoug Sandom – drums (1964)\nKeith Moon – drums (1964–1978)\n[url=/artist/176121-Kenny-Jones]Kenney Jones[/url] – drums (1978–1988)\n\n[i]Touring band musicians:[/i]\n[a=John \"Rabbit\" Bundrick] – keyboards (1979–1981, 1989, 1996–2011)\n[a=Howie Casey] – saxophone (1979–1980)\n[a=Dick Parry] – saxophone (1979–1980)\n[a=Dave Caswell] – trumpet (1979–1980)\n[a=Reg Brooks] – trombone (1979–1980)\n[a=Tim Gorman] – keyboards (1982)\n[url=/artist/324401-Steve-Bolton]Steve \"Boltz\" Bolton[/url] – guitar (1989)\n[a=Simon Phillips] – drums (1989)\n[a=Jody Linscott] – percussion (1989, 1996–1997, 2025)\n[a=Chyna] – backing vocals (1989)\n[a=Cleveland Watkiss] – backing vocals (1989)\n[a=Billy Nicholls] – backing vocals, musical director (1989, 1996–1997, 2019–2025)\n[a=The Kick Horns]: [a=Simon Gardner], [a=Neil Sidwell], [a=Simon Clarke (2)], [a=Tim Sanders], [a=Roddy Lorimer] (1989)\n[a=Simon Townshend] – guitar (1996–1997, 2002–2025)\n[a=Zak Starkey] – drums (1996–2025)\n[a=Jon Carin] – keyboards (1996–1997)\n[a=Dennis Farias] – trumpet (1996–1997)\n[a=Roy Wiegand] – trumpet (1996–1997)\n[a=Nick Lane] – trombone (1996–1997)\n[a=Pino Palladino] – bass (2002–2015)\n[a=Brian Kehew] – keyboards (2006–2007)\n[a=Morgan Nicholls] – bass (2012)\n[a=Chris Stainton] – keyboards (2012)\n[a=Frank Simes] – guitar, musical director (2012–2017)\n[a=John Corey] – keyboards (2012–2017)\n[a=Loren Gold] – keyboards (2012–2025)\n[a=Dylan Hart] – horns (2012–2013)\n[a=Reggie Grisham] – horns (2012–2013)\n[url=/artist/3236345-JG-Miller]J. Greg Miller[/url] – horns (2012–2013)\n[a=Jon Button] – bass guitar (2017–2025)\n[a=Keith Levenson] – conductor (2019–2024)\n[a=Audrey Q. Snyder] – cello (2019–2024)\n[a=Emily Marshall (2)] – keyboards, conductor (2019–2024)\n[a=Katie Jacoby] – violin (2019–2025)\n[a=Randy Landau] – contrabass (2022–2024)\n[a=Scott Devours] – drums (2025)\n[a=John Hogg] – backing vocals (2025)","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:196766","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":280864,"name":"The High Numbers"}],"groups":null,"m